# Project agent guidance

## Purpose and status

Maintain the offline Windows x64 12daz/12da to DXF 2018 converter. The first published Rust pre-release is v0.2.0. Use docs/rust_migration_validation.md for actual test evidence. RUST-17 was disabled for the migration run at the maintainer's request and is tracked separately for clean-machine verification.

## Source of truth

- readme.md: overview and current status.
- rust/Cargo.toml defines the active application version, dependencies, and Windows desktop feature.
- rust/src contains the Rust archive reader, parser, geometry routines, preview, DXF backend and conversion service. rust/src/main.rs is the CLI and rust/src/desktop is the native GUI.
- 12daz_design.md describes the archive and conversion behaviour. docs/test_requirements.md defines the product requirements.
- design.md and docs/background_map_test_requirements.md describe the Rust desktop background map integration and its test cases.
- docs/rust_migration_plan.md, docs/rust_migration_test_requirements.md, and docs/rust_migration_validation.md record the migration and its qualification.
- src/az12_converter and docs/rust_migration_baseline.md preserve the historical Python reference for parity tests and earlier release tags.
- website/ contains the product pages and public download links.

## README maintenance

Keep the overview and **Project documents** section in `readme.md` current. When adding, moving, renaming, superseding or removing a project document, update the README links and descriptions in the same change. Review the relevant README sections whenever features, setup steps, validation status or releases change. Before finishing, check that local links resolve and that descriptions reflect the current source and published release.

## Markdown document structure

Number sections in project Markdown documents. Leave the document title unnumbered. Start main sections at `1.`, `2.`, and so on. Number subsections by their parent section, such as `1.1`, `1.2`, and `2.1`. When headings are added, removed, or moved, update the numbering and check any links to affected headings.

## Agreed scope

Implement future features and enhancements in Rust only. Keep `src/az12_converter` as the historical Python parity reference. Add tests for new behaviour to the Rust suite.

Use Rust for the converter, eframe/egui for the desktop, and acadrust for DXF 2018. Keep conversion independent of the GUI. Export full survey XY and use zero elevation in Flatten 2D. Keep Z requires verified source elevations. Preserve coordinates when declaring drawing units. Report unsupported objects, approximations and substitutions. DWG and drainage Keep Z remain outside the current supported scope.

## Implementation guidance

Keep archive reading, grammar parsing, geometry, preview, DXF and desktop code in their existing modules. Stream input within configured limits and run jobs through the shared conversion service. The desktop worker uses revision checks so stale previews cannot replace the current selection. Validate the temporary DXF before atomic publication and protect the source and existing destinations on failure or cancellation.

## Testing and reporting

Use the case IDs in docs/test_requirements.md and docs/rust_migration_test_requirements.md. Run these commands from the repository root on Windows.

```powershell
cargo test --manifest-path rust/Cargo.toml --locked --features desktop
.\scripts\build_rust_windows.ps1
.\scripts\test_rust_windows_package.ps1 -Archive .\dist\rust-candidate\12dAZ-Converter-0.3.0-Windows-x64.zip
```

Use Python 3.11 with the pinned ezdxf version for the independent audit and parity tests. Set `RUST_MIGRATION_CLI` to the built CLI and run `python -m unittest discover -s tests -p test_rust_migration.py -v`. Record executed results, CAD interoperability, package evidence and benchmark hardware. Use .github/workflows/test.yml for Windows release builds and docs/github_release.md for tagging and signing steps. Change public version and download links after verifying the corresponding release assets.

## Repository hygiene

Keep private survey fixtures in ignored sample/ and generated conversion output in ignored outputs/. Commit synthetic fixtures only with redistribution permission; update ignore rules narrowly if needed for approved fixture paths. Do not commit secrets, environments, build output or private surveys. Preserve user changes and keep documentation aligned with implemented scope.

## GUI theme and preview

Preserve the dark grey theme, blue primary actions and three-panel layout. Share the 30,000-point preview allowance across selected models and regenerate it in the background after selection changes. Never apply a stale preview job result or limit DXF output to display geometry.

Get maintainer approval for the revised GUI before replacing its public sample on the website. Use an actual screenshot with synthetic demonstration data.

## GUI help

Implement a Help menu with bundled offline User Guide, About, and License and Third-party Notices. About must show the installed version/build and the exact notice: Copyright (c) 2026 Lambda Technologies Pty Ltd ABN 22 647 165 160. Keep notices consistent with LICENSE and test UI-07/UI-08.

## Optional support

Use only the owner-supplied public support URL, https://paypal.me/12dazapp. Support is optional. Show the brief dismissible splash only for normal GUI startup; never open the browser automatically or show the splash in CLI/worker/smoke-test paths. Keep the same link in Help > About and the README.

## License

Project source and documentation use the MIT license in LICENSE. Preserve third-party license notices. This project license does not grant redistribution rights to third-party dependencies or private input data.
